      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i have a physics exam coming up and i'm struggling a bit with the concept of quantum entanglement. can you give me a concise, non-mathematical summary of what it actually is and how it's different from just regular classical correlation? thanks",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Quantum entanglement is a phenomenon that's a bit like a mysterious, invisible thread that connects two particles in a way that's hard to wrap your head around. Think of it like a pair of twins who are separated at birth, but somehow, they're still connected in a way that lets them influence each other, even if they're on opposite sides of the universe. \n\nIn classical physics, correlation between two particles is like a game of telephone - the information gets passed from one particle to the other, but it's a one-way street. But with entanglement, it's more like a two-way conversation, where the particles are \"talking\" to each other in a way that's faster than the speed of light. \n\nImagine two coins, one in New York and one in Los Angeles. In classical physics, if you flip the coin in New York, it won't instantly affect the coin in Los Angeles. But with entanglement, it's like the two coins are connected by a magical spring, so that if you flip the one in New York, the other one will instantly flip too, no matter how far apart they are. \n\nThe key difference is that entanglement is a fundamental property of quantum mechanics",
      "source": "target",
